# shir_nodes/deref.node — Deref: read through a reference (`*p`).
#
# GENERIC (Go *p auto-deref reads, Zig p.*, C-like *p). Matching read
# side of AddressOf (see address_of.node for the pair contract):
#   - reference-capable backends render a true dereference
#     (Perl: ${...} / @{...});
#   - value-only backends pass the operand through (snapshot semantics;
#     nil-deref detection stays with guards the frontend already emits,
#     e.g. `p == nil`).
# WRITES through a dereference have no node here by design: they are a
# storage effect, refused by frontends rather than guessed.

node Deref
tag "Deref"
kind expr
# storage_class str_bounded
field pointer: expr
# Spec: TextOps#deref_sound (docs/fm/TextOps/ShIR_TextOps_Strings.thy, section
# "CountedFor, Spread, AddressOf/Deref, CloneDeep: value-level laws"). The read half of
# the pair contract: dereferencing an address yields the cell's stored bytes, so a write
# through that address followed by a dereference yields the stored value; and writing a
# DIFFERENT cell does not disturb this one. The same theorem carries the value-only
# backends' arm (the operand passes through, snapshot semantics).
#
# NOT PROMISED (stated, not assumed): nil-dereference detection (the frontend's `p == nil`
# guards own it); WRITES through a dereference, which this node deliberately has no form
# for; allocation/freshness; aliasing; and lifetime/GC.
spec: TextOps#deref_sound
